Match JavaScript URIs case-insensitively and make <script> element removal
namespace-aware.

CVE-2026-73650 / GHSA-2p49-hgcm-8545: the removeScriptElement plugin only
stripped elements literally named "script" and only matched "javascript:"
URIs case-sensitively. An attacker could bypass sanitization with a
namespace-prefixed script element declared in the SVG/XHTML namespace
(e.g. <svg:script>) or with a mixed/upper-case URI scheme
(e.g. "JAVASCRIPT:"), leaving executable content in the "optimized" output.

This backports upstream commit 72a23886b4698b27624b936f3a15a80afd36d75f:
 - lib/svgo/tools.js: lowercase the URI before the "javascript:" prefix check.
 - plugins/removeScriptElement.js: track xmlns: prefix namespace stacks and
   remove <*:script> elements only when the prefix resolves to the SVG or
   XHTML namespace; lowercase href values before the "javascript:" check.
 - test fixtures 06/07/08 covering the new behaviour.

